Import "../Chemistry.lingua"

Describe phases components and the constraints on a material sample (@chemistry-phase-description: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Derive-a-pure-substance-coexistence-slope-from-chemical-potentials.lingua#r_00F3CC35D72514466233FCB96F") { r_00B2D4B910F9BD44A183D65846
A sample can contain several regions with different compositions or structures while having one overall elemental account. A phase is a macroscopically homogeneous part with its own intensive properties and state model; separate droplets with the same properties do not count as different phases merely because they are disconnected. A component is an independent substance basis sufficient for the composition account. In the nonreacting binary cases below, neutral substances A and B are the two components. Reactions, ions and other constraints can change that count.

Reuse [Interpret vapor pressure, phase change, and a phase diagram](../general-chemistry/states/Phase-changes-and-equilibrium-maps.lingua#r_7XXDG9YQTWEGSDFX1HXPBTGY8S) and [Explain solution formation through interactions and thermodynamic conditions](../general-chemistry/solutions/Solvation-mixing-and-solubility.lingua#r_4PY91PEG2715V7F8H7MVGP4S95). A visible interface, a cloudy sample, a cooling arrest and a clear solution are observations to interpret, not complete equilibrium proofs. The same composition can have different molecular arrangements, crystal structures, metastable states or gradients. [Review molecular identity through representations transformations and evidence](Review-molecular-identity-through-representations-transformations-and-evidence.lingua#r_0076E2B38375A5256FCEB27DAE) supplies identity and structural evidence; a phase diagram adds state and equilibrium conditions rather than replacing chemical identity.

Specify temperature T, pressure p, conserved amounts, permitted exchanges and the candidate phases. Here use bulk phases at common T,p, no reactions, negligible interfacial contribution to total Gibbs energy, and no additional elastic/electrical work coordinates. Under the no-extracted-work environment of [Choose chemical constraints before using a free energy sign](Choose-chemical-constraints-before-using-a-free-energy-sign.lingua#r_0024B2FCA41783BA78949E37E5), equilibrium minimizes total G over permitted changes. A partition preventing component transfer changes that optimization. Finite droplets, coherently strained solids or charged interfaces may require extra terms and conditions.

For a generic nonreacting C-component, P-phase equilibrium with every component transferable, count 2+P(C−1) intensive variables: common T,p and C−1 independent fractions per phase. Equality of each component's chemical potential across phases supplies C(P−1) independent constraints, giving F=C−P+2. This Gibbs phase-rule count assumes the stated variables and independent constraints; it is not an existence proof for every algebraically allowed collection of phases. Amounts of phases are extensive and are not counted in F. Exceptional constraints, reactions or dependent conditions require a revised count.

A pure substance with two phases has F=1: coexistence traces a curve in T,p. A binary two-phase system has F=2; fixing T,p generally fixes the phase compositions, while changing the conserved overall composition changes their amounts. At a pure-substance triple point F=0, though the phase amounts can still vary under material/energy constraints. A two-phase region on a binary composition diagram is not the same geometrical object as a coexistence curve on a pure-substance T,p diagram.

**Work:** describe water vapor above a liquid, a saturated solution with solid present, and a hypothetical two-liquid sample using phase identities, overall composition and allowed exchanges. Give two reasons a cloudy observation may not establish equilibrium coexistence. Derive the nonreacting phase-rule count and distinguish its intensive degrees of freedom from the number of unknown phase amounts. Read [Thermodynamics and Chemistry — Howard DeVoe](../../physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/sources/Thermodynamics-and-Chemistry-DeVoe.lingua#r_2YHHGY26Y8X9MS9DB3MXZHD1D1) sections 8.1–8.2 and 13.1 progressively; broader reactive cases remain later work.

**Continue:** [Derive a pure substance coexistence slope from chemical potentials](Derive-a-pure-substance-coexistence-slope-from-chemical-potentials.lingua#r_00F3CC35D72514466233FCB96F).

